        "scanners": {
          "vt": null,
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill's goal (multi‑agent orchestration) matches the files and scripts provided, but the runtime instructions and bundled scripts grant broad, high‑privilege changes to the user's OpenClaw environment (writing configs, creating symlinks, installing a background watcher, modifying openclaw.json to allow subagents, and restarting the Gateway) while not declaring required credentials — review before running.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The name/description (multi‑agent orchestration) aligns with the included assets: agent templates, project.json, many AGENTS.md/SOUL.md/IDENTITY.md files and orchestration scripts. Creating workspaces, agent configs, and a watcher is coherent for this purpose. However, the skill performs high‑impact actions (writing ~/.openclaw/*, symlinks, adding agents to openclaw.json and setting main.subagents.allowAgents:[\"*\"], restarting the Gateway) that materially change the host agent environment; those are powerful operations that should be explicitly documented and consented to.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md explicitly instructs the agent to run setup.sh immediately (\"must first execute\") and describes automated creation/copying of directories, templates, per‑agent agentDir copies, creation of ~/.openclaw/configs/team-dispatch.json, modification of openclaw.json, and a Gateway restart. These instructions go beyond passive guidance and give the agent permission to modify persistent configuration and system behavior; they also install and recommend a background watcher. The instructions therefore grant broad write/privilege scope over the agent runtime and system scheduler (launchd/systemd/cron) which is high‑impact and outside simple answer/analysis behavior.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "There is no formal install spec in registry metadata (instruction‑only), but the package includes many shell and Python scripts (scripts/setup.sh, watch-install.sh, watch.sh, backups.sh, etc.) that are intended to be executed. Bundling scripts inside the skill and instructing an agent to run them effectively functions as an installer; since the code is local (no remote download URLs shown in the truncated listing) this is lower risk than fetching arbitrary remote archives, but running these scripts will write/modify files and restart services — review the scripts before execution.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Registry metadata declares no required environment variables or primary credential, but the docs/scripts reference model providers and token behavior (openai provider, OPENAI_API_KEY mentioned in troubleshooting) and Telegram notification configuration. The skill will interact with model providers and optionally Telegram if configured, yet it does not declare those credentials as required — this is an inconsistency. Also, writing openclaw.json to set main.subagents.allowAgents:[\"*\"] expands agent permissions and could be disproportionate unless explicitly needed and reviewed.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill writes persistent configuration (symlink ~/.openclaw/skills/team-dispatch → <SKILL_DIR>), creates ~/.openclaw/workspace/tasks/, copies agent templates to ~/.openclaw/agents/<id>, creates ~/.openclaw/configs/team-dispatch.json, modifies agents.list in openclaw.json, and restarts the Gateway. It also installs/instantiates a background watcher (openclaw-cron / launchd / systemd / cron). While always:false (not force‑enabled globally), these changes grant long‑lived privileges and modify the agent environment and scheduling; that persistent footprint is significant and should be consented to and audited.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
